[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. That is true for most
reports, but not for all of them. Read one by one, the merged reports hold **three problems**,
and each needs its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. Fixing the parser closes the first problem only. The other two stay open
and nobody is working on them.
</Callout>

## How many reports each problem covers

<ProblemCounts ticket={4471} />

## The problems

1. **`parser` — the shared parser reads dates month first.** Day-first tenants (every tenant in
   the export writes the day first) see day and month swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July. This is
   the bug #4471 describes. It covers the large majority of the reports.
2. **`hint` — booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field.** Tenants whose people write
   day first (alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour) type what the hint tells them, month
   first, and the form reads it their way. The symptom looks the same as `parser`. The cause is
   the form, so a parser fix does not touch it. These reports come in through the `form` channel.
3. **`one-day-move` — a booking lands one day off, not a month flipped.** "Tuesday 6 October
   showed as Monday", "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday", "the invite
   arrived before I made the booking". This looks like a time zone problem. The desk read these
   five reports as a one-day move itself (`desk-notes.json`), and no month is swapped in them.

## What the intake tag hides

Intake guesses `parser` or `hint` from the reporter's browser. The guess is wrong for 38 reports:
the table below, built from the tenant's locale and booking form, shows the tags that fall under
each cause. Count by tenant, never by the tag.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why one ticket was wrong

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line. The subjects
here ("Calendar invite is wrong", "Dates mixed up") are shared by reports with different causes,
so subject matching put three problems under one number. The decision stays as written; a
later record should replace it.

Four reports are too vague to place by their words (a request for a day-first setting, "the wrong
day" for a parent, a client told the date in words, an October-for-January swap). They are
counted under `parser` by tenant, as the desk does.

